---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import { ROLES } from '../lib/taxonomy';
import { REPO_URL } from '../site.config';

const template = `---
title: "Example title"
description: "Short description"
roles:
  - software-engineer
tags:
  - cpp
  - cuda
  - gpu
  - profiling
author: "@exampleuser"
organisation: "Example University"
date: 2026-08-26
timeSensitive: false
sources:
  - title: "NVIDIA Nsight Compute documentation"
    url: "https://docs.nvidia.com/nsight-compute/"
---

## What we were trying to solve

One or two paragraphs of context...`;
---

<Base
  title="Contribute"
  description="How to submit a case study: add one Markdown file, open a pull request, answer the template questions."
>
  <div class="wrap section">
    <div class="section__head section__head--wide">
      <div>
        <h1>Contributing</h1>
        <p> Share your experiences of working on sustainability in your role. </p>
        <p> This site is only useful if it reflects what people are really doing, including the things that did not work!
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="prose prose--wide">
      <h2>How to submit an article</h2>
      <ol>
        <li>Fork the repository and create a branch.</li>
        <li>
          Copy <code>templates/article-template.md</code> to
          <code>content/case-studies/your-article-name.md</code>. The filename becomes the
          article's web address, so use lowercase words separated by hyphens.
        </li>
        <li>Fill in the YAML header and write the body in Markdown (see Article format below).</li>
        <li>
          Open a pull request, answering the few questions in the description.
        </li>
        <li>
          A maintainer reviews as a quality check. 
        </li>
      </ol>
      <p>
      If you haven't got a fully formed idea, or you need help with the process,  
         <a href={`${REPO_URL}/issues/new/choose`} rel="noopener"> open an issue</a> to get feedback.
      </p>

      <h2>Article format</h2>
      <p>
      Each article is a single Markdown file with a YAML header. </p>
      <p>The header is used to ensure all required fields are filled when 
        making a pull request.
      </p>
      <pre><code>{template}</code></pre>

      <h3>Fields</h3>
      <table>
        <thead>
          <tr><th>Field</th><th>Required</th><th>Notes</th></tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td><code>title</code></td><td>Yes</td><td>8–100 characters.</td></tr>
          <tr>
            <td><code>description</code></td><td>Yes</td>
            <td>40–300 characters. Shown on cards and in search results.</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td><code>roles</code></td><td>Yes</td>
            <td>1–4 from the fixed list below.</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td><code>tags</code></td><td>Yes</td>
            <td>1–8, lowercase kebab-case. Reuse existing tags where you can.</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td><code>author</code></td><td>Yes</td>
            <td>Your GitHub handle, or a list of handles for co-authors.</td>
          </tr>
          <tr><td><code>organisation</code></td><td>No</td><td>Institution or facility.</td></tr>
          <tr><td><code>date</code></td><td>Yes</td><td><code>YYYY-MM-DD</code>.</td></tr>
          <tr>
            <td><code>updated</code></td><td>No</td>
            <td>Set on substantive revisions. Must not precede <code>date</code>.</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td><code>timeSensitive</code></td><td>No</td>
            <td>
              <code>true</code> if the article cites prices, tool versions or draft policy. Renders
              a dated warning banner.
            </td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td><code>sources</code></td><td>No</td>
            <td>Title/URL pairs. Strongly encouraged, especially for any figures you quote.</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td><code>draft</code></td><td>No</td>
            <td><code>true</code> keeps it out of the published site while you work.</td>
          </tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>

      <h2>Roles</h2>
      <p>Who is this for? Pick the roles that would be most interested in your contribution.</p>
      <ul>
        {
          Object.entries(ROLES).map(([id, role]) => (
            <li>
              <code>{id}</code>: {role.description}
            </li>
          ))
        }
      </ul>

      <h2>What makes a good article</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>
          Be specific. Often sustainabiity advice is less practical because it is too generic. What is relevant to your actual role?
        </li>
        <li>
          Say what it cost. Effort, money, disruption, etc.
        </li>
        <li>
          Include anything that did not work.
        </li>
        <li>
          Cite any sources
        </li>
        <li>
          Only submit content you wrote or have the right to
          publish. Everything here is released under CC&nbsp;BY&nbsp;4.0.
        </li>
      </ul>

      <h2>Commenting</h2>
      <p>
        Every article has a discussion thread using GitHub Discussions. Sign in with a GitHub
        account to add a question/comment.
      </p>

    </div>
  </div>
</Base>
